  {
   "id": "nAuaAi70AwAJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2016-04-15T15:56:20Z",
   "subject": "Re: _NEW_ term when using stan_glmer",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Friday, April 15, 2016 at 10:11:58 AM UTC-4, Paul Scutti wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I\n am pretty new to rstanarm and multilevel modelling (and this might \nsound pretty silly to you), but in some instances when running a \nstan_glmer model I have come across a _NEW_ intercept in the output (see\n below in bold). What is this term exactly?</div></blockquote><br></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">It\n is the posterior distribution distribution of the parameter(s) for a \nnew level of the grouping term(s). In your case, if there were an eighth\n SUPPLIER1, it would have an intercept shift that is normal with mean \nzero and standard deviation 0.123.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "GbcWZq45BAAJ",
   "from": "Paul Scutti",
   "author": "50376cb8eda8eca6",
   "date": "2016-04-16T13:09:57Z",
   "subject": "Re: _NEW_ term when using stan_glmer",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Thanks for that Ben. Is there any documentation you can refer me to? I&#39;d like to know more about the reasoning behind this.</div><div><br></div><div>Paul</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><br><br>On Friday, April 15, 2016 at 4:56:20 PM UTC+1, Ben Goodrich wrote:</div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Friday, April 15, 2016 at 10:11:58 AM UTC-4, Paul Scutti wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I\n am pretty new to rstanarm and multilevel modelling (and this might \nsound pretty silly to you), but in some instances when running a \nstan_glmer model I have come across a _NEW_ intercept in the output (see\n below in bold). What is this term exactly?</div></blockquote><br>It\n is the posterior distribution distribution of the parameter(s) for a \nnew level of the grouping term(s). In your case, if there were an eighth\n SUPPLIER1, it would have an intercept shift that is normal with mean \nzero and standard deviation 0.123.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ej5Q9xg_BAAJ",
   "from": "Jonah Gabry",
   "author": "fc5d3270037d7478",
   "date": "2016-04-16T14:49:13Z",
   "subject": "Re: _NEW_ term when using stan_glmer",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Paul, <div><br></div><div>We didn&#39;t put out any user-facing documentation for this because we didn&#39;t want people to focus on this too much, but perhaps it would be better to include something about this in the package documentation. I suppose the best place would probably be in the documentation for the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">posterior_predict </font><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">function.</font><br><div><br></div><div>Following up on what Ben said, the reason we add the terms labeled <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">_NEW_</font> is so that we can allow <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">posterior_predict</font> to accept data (the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">newdata</font> argument) containing levels of grouping variables not present in the original data. We mostly try to hide these <span style=\"font-family:&#39;courier new&#39;,monospace\">_NEW_</span> terms from the user because they&#39;re not really of direct interest but rather just used internally to be able to produce the predictions if necessary. </div><div><br></div><div>Essentially, we add extra parameters to the group-level parameters but we make sure they do not enter the likelihood. That way the estimates of the actual group-level parameters are the same as they would be if we didn&#39;t add the extra levels, but we can use the posterior distribution of the extra levels if we need them when making predictions. Even though they don&#39;t enter the likelihood their posterior will <i>not</i> be identical to their prior because the parameters for the new levels share common hyperparameters with the other group-level parameters and we learn something about these hyperparameters. So we&#39;re updating what we believe about the new levels based on what we&#39;ve learned from the data we did observe.</div><div><br></div><div>Hope that helps,</div><div><br></div><div>Jonah</div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "WKb4des_BAAJ",
   "from": "Paul Scutti",
   "author": "50376cb8eda8eca6",
   "date": "2016-04-16T15:04:17Z",
   "subject": "Re: _NEW_ term when using stan_glmer",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Jonah,</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for the clarification. That makes perfect sense!</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Paul</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><br><br>On Saturday, April 16, 2016 at 3:49:13 PM UTC+1, Jonah Gabry wrote:</div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Paul, <div><br></div><div>We didn&#39;t put out any user-facing documentation for this because we didn&#39;t want people to focus on this too much, but perhaps it would be better to include something about this in the package documentation. I suppose the best place would probably be in the documentation for the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">posterior_predict </font><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">function.</font><br><div><br></div><div>Following up on what Ben said, the reason we add the terms labeled <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">_NEW_</font> is so that we can allow <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">posterior_predict</font> to accept data (the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">newdata</font> argument) containing levels of grouping variables not present in the original data. We mostly try to hide these <span style=\"font-family:&#39;courier new&#39;,monospace\">_NEW_</span> terms from the user because they&#39;re not really of direct interest but rather just used internally to be able to produce the predictions if necessary. </div><div><br></div><div>Essentially, we add extra parameters to the group-level parameters but we make sure they do not enter the likelihood. That way the estimates of the actual group-level parameters are the same as they would be if we didn&#39;t add the extra levels, but we can use the posterior distribution of the extra levels if we need them when making predictions. Even though they don&#39;t enter the likelihood their posterior will <i>not</i> be identical to their prior because the parameters for the new levels share common hyperparameters with the other group-level parameters and we learn something about these hyperparameters. So we&#39;re updating what we believe about the new levels based on what we&#39;ve learned from the data we did observe.</div><div><br></div><div>Hope that helps,</div><div><br></div><div>Jonah</div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
